    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3677.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 813,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**—meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The Phases of Water)\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** The fluid we drink, swim in, and use to wash.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, fog, or steam rising from a boiling pot.\n\n### 2. Why Water is Unique (Scientific Wonders)\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals). Because of this, it acts as a transport medium, carrying nutrients and chemicals through living organisms and rivers.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is different. When water freezes, its molecules lock into a structure that takes up *more* space, making ice less dense than liquid water. This is why ice cubes float. In nature, this means lakes freeze from the top down, allowing fish and aquatic life to survive the winter underneath the ice.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This is why the oceans help regulate the temperature of the entire planet, keeping coastal areas from experiencing extreme temperature swings.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which is how water climbs up the roots of giant trees).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of Earth’s surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite how vast the oceans look, **96.5% of Earth's water is saltwater** (undrinkable without desalination). Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and soil.\n*   **The Water Cycle:** Water is endlessly recycled on Earth through evaporation (turning into gas from the heat of the sun), condensation (forming clouds), precipitation (rain and snow), and collection (flowing back into rivers and oceans). The water you drink today is the exact same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### 4. Water and Life\nBiologically, water is the cradle of life. \n*   **In Nature:** Every known living thing—from bacteria and plants to blue whales and humans—requires water to survive. \n*   **In Humans:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, our lungs about 83%, and even our bones are about 31% water. It regulates our internal body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and carries oxygen through our blood. \n\nIn summary, water is the driving force of nature, weather, climate, and biology. Without it, life as we know it would simply not exist."
